claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> Some patches on the list target the mail branch, and they belong in
> the mail workspace, which is a jj workspace with no .git of its own.
> git am needs a repo, so a patch piped from notmuch there fails.
>
> Pointing git am at the trunk workspace's .git does not help. It
> commits on top of that repo's HEAD, with trunk's tree under the
> patch, and jj then moves the trunk workspace onto those commits.
> Checking out mail in the trunk workspace instead is slow and drags
> along the build output trunk leaves behind.
>
> am.sh does what git am does, in whatever jj workspace it runs in.
> git still parses the mail and applies the diff, since neither needs a
> repo, and jj records each patch as a change with its author, date,
> and message.
> ---
> change-id: mylnzmrtzvwwzusstlqttzxtpkuumwrl
>
> Tested on a scratch colocated repo with a second workspace that has
> no .git. A series thread piped as one mbox (cover letter, two
> patches, a review reply) applied the two patches as two changes
> carrying each patch's author and date, and skipped the rest. A
> patch that does not apply, or a non-empty @, stopped with nothing
> changed. Like git am, the applied changes get new change ids; the
> Change-Id header is not carried over.
>
>  libexec/mail/am.sh | 93 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
>  1 file changed, 93 insertions(+)
>  create mode 100755 libexec/mail/am.sh

Good start but I think it's doing too much.

I want the interface to be `just am <workspace>=default` and have it
receive stdin.

I'm okay with there being a helper script or whatever, but I'll be
calling it from `just am` so that I can run it from the default repo. It
reads from stdin so that I can use | from notmuch-emacs to pipe the
current message and apply it.

Also it doesn't need confirmation output etc, it should work similarly
to git-am and most other tools in that there's no output if it succeeds.
